<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jianzhou Jurchens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jianzhou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> were one of the three main groups into which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> divided the Jurchen peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, living in the mountains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. From 1583 their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> united the Jianzhou and then the other Jurchen groups, organising them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> and founding the Later Jin in 1616. In 1635 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> renamed the Jurchens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, and the state became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens.jpg" alt="Jianzhou Jurchens" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Herrmann Creator:Q1509115 &amp; Georg Westermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ming_Chinese_empire_1415_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
